WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Speaker 1: Hey, this is really good&nbsp;
news. Democrats have unveiled a bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:14.320
proposing full decriminalization of drug&nbsp;
possession, as well as expunging the records of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:21.120
many who have already been convicted along those&nbsp;
lines. And we've been talking about this for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.600
very long time. We have talked about the racist&nbsp;
origins of the war on drugs, which I will get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.280
in a moment. We've talked about a lot of&nbsp;
other issues related to this, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:41.040
the way that police departments defend unenforced,&nbsp;
depend on sorry, depend on enforcing many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.000
laws in order to sustain themselves financially.&nbsp;
We've talked about private prisons. We've talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.080
about all of these different things. And then&nbsp;
there was a question, well, what are we going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:57.200
see from Democrats under Joe Biden along these&nbsp;
lines? And we have now learned that there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.200
really comprehensive bill that has been proposed.&nbsp;
So let's go through it. Newsweek reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.240
a bill to end criminal penalties for drug&nbsp;
possession at the federal level was unveiled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.800
Democrats Tuesday yesterday ahead of the fiftieth&nbsp;
anniversary of President Richard Nixon declaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.280
the war on drugs, the Drug Policy Reform Act&nbsp;
would decriminalize personal use, possession of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.960
all scheduled drugs, including marijuana, heroin&nbsp;
and cocaine, and automatically expunge records&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.240
and provide for resentencing for those serving&nbsp;
time for certain drug related arrests. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.040
I know many people will say, David, this isn't&nbsp;
this isn't perfect. We need legalization instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.600
of decriminalization and we need to expunge all&nbsp;
we're talking about major progress. If this were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.160
to pass, it's already a major step forward. There&nbsp;
would still be more to do. But let's actually go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.240
let's take these steps. It would also&nbsp;
prohibit the denial of employment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.880
immigration status, public benefits, voting&nbsp;
rights and more based upon criminal history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.960
for drug possession. The legislation proposed&nbsp;
by Representatives Corey Bush of Missouri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.800
and Bonnie Watson Coleman of New Jersey, also&nbsp;
called for the country to address substance abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.840
with more health centered approaches. Very, very&nbsp;
important. June 17th will mark 50 years since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.640
Nixon designated drugs as, quote, America's&nbsp;
public enemy number one. Can you imagine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.720
Since then, drug offenses have become the leading&nbsp;
cause of arrests in the US. According to the Drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:32.160
Policy Alliance, in twenty nineteen, more than one&nbsp;
point five million arrests were made due to drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:40.000
violations under the DPRK. Authority and criteria&nbsp;
for the classification of substances would shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.160
from the U.S. attorney general to the Secretary of&nbsp;
Health and Human Services. This could be a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.440
big deal in terms of actual rescheduling. It's&nbsp;
not just about decriminalization, it's also about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:57.040
rescheduling of drugs. Importantly, the bill would&nbsp;
also direct the HHS to establish a grant program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.680
supporting initiatives that expand access to&nbsp;
substance abuse treatment. This is really simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:09.280
guys. This is we should treat this issue like&nbsp;
a public health issue, not a criminal issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.200
And if you go back to the origins of the war on&nbsp;
drugs and then you look at the genesis and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.440
catalysts that led to it, you end up understanding&nbsp;
also, by the way, if you go back even further to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.240
prohibition of alcohol. Early in the 20th century,&nbsp;
the enforcement of the prohibition of these drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.160
may well lead to more crime than it prevents.&nbsp;
I don't think it's exaggerated to say that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.760
you would have thought maybe we learned that&nbsp;
during alcohol prohibition in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.400
there were decades between that and Nixon&nbsp;
launching the war on drugs. So maybe it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:50.400
